    "design_change_disclosure": "carb_sensitivity was initially also a reason (outside training [min, max]). After seeing that it flagged one TEST patient whose sensitivity (0.846) is 0.002 below the training minimum (0.848), it was removed as a reason because a what-if meal does not change it (patient-level, not scenario-level). This choice was informed by test data, so the test flag rates below are not fully untouched evidence.",
    "rule_selection_disclosure": [
      "The bounds are fitted on training meals only and no threshold was tuned on test outcomes, but the rule design was not chosen blind to held-out data:",
      "the calibrated 2-D Mahalanobis/kNN alternative was rejected partly because of its false-warning rate on TEST patients;",
      "carb_sensitivity was removed as a reason after a TEST boundary flag was seen (see above);",
      "the raw 5-D Mahalanobis demonstration used VALIDATION rows;",
      "the reference set changed from CGM feature rows (format 2, which silently missed 25 training meals) to every logged training meal event (format 3) after the format-2 validation/test rates had been seen. The change was prompted by a code review of training-data coverage, not by outcomes, and the coordinates and tolerances were not changed.",
      "Therefore the validation/test flag rates are not untouched held-out estimates of the final rule."
    ],
    "timing_behaviour": "The check is non-monotonic in time since the last logged meal: a meal < 30 min after another merges into 'meal now' (carbs_30), so it is flagged only if the combined grams or expected rise exceed training; 30-60 min after a meal it is always flagged (no training meal had a meal 30-60 min before it, prior_carbs_30_60 max = 0, reflecting the synthetic generator's meal schedule); 60-120 min after, it depends on the earlier meal's size. This is accurate about training support but says nothing specific about forecast error.",
